{"from": "claim:jn-21-01-sea-tiberias-again", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-06-01-sea-called-tiberias", "note": "Same lake, same naming: John 6:1 'the sea of Galilee, which is also called the sea of Tiberias' carries the naming evidence (Josephus, B); 21:1 is a new appearance scene at that lake."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-03-night-fishing-nothing", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-05-catch-of-fish", "note": "Luke 5:5 'we toiled all night and took nothing' inside the pre-Easter catch scene; John 21:3's fruitless night fishing precedes a different, post-resurrection catch. Related scenes, not the same event."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-06-cast-right-side-multitude", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-05-catch-of-fish", "note": "Both are a miraculous catch after a fruitless night, but Luke's is the pre-Easter call of Simon, John's a post-resurrection appearance by the Sea of Tiberias - different occasions, not parallels."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-06-cast-right-side-multitude", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-13-47-dragnet-sea", "note": "Matthew 13:47 states the seine (dragnet) custom of Galilean fishing; John 21 narrates casting a net from a working boat - same fishery, parable custom vs narrative event."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-09-charcoal-fish-bread", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-18-18-fire-cold", "note": "Both verses use the rare word anthrakia ('fire of coals'): the courtyard fire of the denial night warmed, the shore fire of 21:9 cooks the breakfast. John is the only Gospel to use the word."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-14-third-appearance", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-20-19-20-appearance-evening", "note": "John 21:14 counts this as the third appearance: the first was the locked-room evening of 20:19-23."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-14-third-appearance", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-20-26-29-thomas-belief", "note": "John 21:14's count assumes the second appearance, eight days later with Thomas (20:26-29)."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-24-eyewitness-authorship", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-20-30-31-purpose", "note": "The two book-conclusion statements of the Gospel: 20:30-31 states the purpose, 21:24 the eyewitness authorship."}
{"from": "claim:jn-21-25-many-things-unwritten", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-20-30-31-purpose", "note": "The same selection statement in exaggerated form: 'many other things which Jesus did' (20:30) becomes the world could not hold the books (21:25)."}
{"from": "passage:jn-21-13", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:lk-24-42", "note": "The risen Jesus eats fish with the disciples in Luke 24:42-43 (in Jerusalem) and serves bread and fish in John 21:12-13 (by the lake) - a shared motif in two different appearance scenes."}
{"from": "artifact:magdala-fishing-gear", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-06-cast-right-side-multitude", "grade": "C", "note": "A needle and lead weights for repairing and holding down fishing nets, excavated 1970s at Magdala on this lake shore: the net-fishing practice 21:6-11 assumes; it attests the practice, never the catch."}
{"from": "artifact:galilee-boat", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-03-night-fishing-nothing", "grade": "C", "note": "A 1st-century fishing boat (8.2 m) recovered from the lake bed: the kind of working boat the seven entered for the night's fishing; grade C context, no link to Jesus claimed."}
{"from": "observation:galilee-fish-species", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-06-cast-right-side-multitude", "grade": "C", "note": "The lake holds musht (tilapia), sardin, biny and catfish: a real, rich fishery behind 'the multitude of fish'; the observation documents the lake, never the miracle."}
{"from": "observation:galilee-fish-species", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-09-charcoal-fish-bread", "grade": "C", "note": "Fish of this lake (musht among them) were the ordinary local fare: fish cooked on the shore is period-plausible eating, not proof of the scene."}
{"from": "observation:galilee-fish-species", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-11-153-fish", "grade": "C", "note": "The lake's fish include the biny (Jordan barbel), among the largest fish of the fishery: 'great fish' is plausible lake catch; the exact count 153 is unattested."}
{"from": "text:1-clement-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-18-19-peters-death", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception c. 96 CE: Peter 'endured... many labors, and thus having borne his testimony went to his appointed place of glory' - the earliest witness that Peter died a martyr's death; it says nothing of the manner or place, and it reports a tradition, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "text:caius-disputation-proclus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-18-19-peters-death", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception c. 198 CE: Caius can 'point out the trophies of the apostles' at the Vatican - the Roman grave-tradition for Peter, which presupposes his Roman death; it attests the tradition, never the manner predicted in 21:18-19."}
{"from": "text:irenaeus-heresies-3-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-24-eyewitness-authorship", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception c. 180 CE: 'Afterwards, John, the disciple of the Lord, who also had leaned upon His breast, did himself publish a Gospel during his residence at Ephesus in Asia' - Irenaeus identifies the author with the one who leaned on Jesus' breast (John 13:23, the beloved disciple); a tradition reporting a tradition, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "text:papias-fragments-john", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-24-eyewitness-authorship", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception c. 100-130 CE: Papias inquired after 'what Andrew or what Peter said, or... what was said by... John', from 'the presbyter John, the disciples of the Lord'; whether his John is the apostle or a distinct 'John the Elder' is disputed (Eusebius distinguished them), so at most it attests that early tradition reached eyewitnesses including a John."}
{"from": "text:jerome-on-153-fish", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-21-11-153-fish", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception: Jerome wrote 'Those who have written about animated nature say that there are a hundred and fifty-three kinds of fish. One of each of these kinds was caught by the Apostle' - an allegorical reading of the count, known only through a Lapide's commentary; it attests the interpretation, never the count."}
